    "result": "I found no defects in this diff.\n\nThe refactor keeps the old behavior:\n\n- **`Approve`** (`ApprovalService.cs:36`): `Decide` sets the same three fields as before (`Status`, `DecidedBy`, `DecidedAt`) and then calls `_repository.Update`. It still doesn't touch `DecisionNote`, as before.\n- **`Reject`** (`ApprovalService.cs:49`): `DecisionNote` is now set before `Decide` instead of after the status change. Both writes land on the same `invoice` object before the single `Update` call, so nothing outside the method can see the new order. If `Update` threw, the in-memory object would already be mutated in either version.\n- **`Decide`** (`ApprovalService.cs:52`): `Find` and `EnsurePending` still run in the callers, before any mutation. The reason validation above line 46 is unchanged, so the guard order is the same.\n\n**Confidence:** high for the changed lines. I only reviewed the diff, not the rest of `ApprovalService.cs` or the tests. I didn't check whether any test asserts `DecisionNote` is null after `Approve`, or runs against a repository that copies invoices instead of returning the same reference. Neither case is affected by this change.",
